{"type": "text", "text": "\n\nThe retained evidence establishes a successful bounded coexistence test, not qualification for everyday unattended monitoring. Three genuine scheduler executions completed endpoint collection and forced subscription interpretation; one overlapped a useful Hermes file-assessment task. It does not establish sustained concurrency, dependable one-minute cadence, automatic provider selection, or monitoring behavior during outages.\n\nSource notation below:\nA = /var/home/mythos/Eregion/Arda/docs/audits/inference-assessment-2026-10-09/\nP = /var/home/mythos/Eregion/Arda/docs/plans/INFERENCE_ASSESSMENT.md\nAggregate = A/installed-operating-acceptance-final.json\n\nAll operating observations below are historical. I read files and calculated timestamp correlations only; I did not execute the qualification scripts, change configuration, delegate, or probe endpoints.\n\nWhat concurrent operation actually passed\n\nThe aggregate’s hermes record shows successful missing-file recovery, source reads, report writing and final completion using openai_sub/gpt-6-astra. Its reported task duration was 69.366 seconds; the enclosing started_at/finished_at interval was 18:25:52.418441–18:27:03.959157 UTC on October 11, or 71.540716 seconds.\n\nThe middle scheduled cycle ran 18:26:48.211302–18:26:54.054987 UTC, entirely inside that interval: 5.843685 seconds of client-interval overlap. The other two cycles had zero overlap. This confirms that background collection and interpretation completed while the Hermes task remained in progress, not that both upstream inference computations were simultaneous.\n\nThe foreground task was deliberately restricted: A/installed-hermes-acceptance.py:5–24 creates a temporary HERMES_HOME, selects the subscription model explicitly, and enables only file tools with a 12-turn/180-second budget. It was not the full active-profile workload, an ongoing operator conversation, or a desktop/gaming latency test.\n\nThe collector was genuinely bounded. Aggregate cycles[].result.collector.unit_properties records:\n\n  CPUQuotaPerSecUSec = 250ms\n  MemoryMax = 134217728\n  MemorySwapMax = 0\n  TasksMax = 16\n  TimeoutStartUSec = 25s\n  TimeoutStopUSec = 5s\n\nIts three reported service runtimes were 499, 464 and 336 milliseconds. Desktop mesh-llm readbacks remained MainPID=0, ActiveState=inactive. These caps apply to the transient collector, not the resident inference services or the entire remote-interpretation workflow.\n\nScheduler provenance is real; regular cadence is not established\n\nI normalized the scheduler’s -07:00 timestamps to UTC and cross-checked all three cycle intervals against their execution records. Each cycle is enclosed by exactly its cited execution, and each saved cron output’s packet and answer matches its cycle.\n\nAll times below are October 11, UTC:\n\n  Execution 116e8bf2ea7d42eebf06eb056d4b8f14\n    Scheduled: 18:19:35.623573\n    Execution: 18:19:48.107143–18:19:55.086978\n    Cycle:     18:19:48.208271–18:19:55.059613\n    Start lateness: 12.483570 seconds\n\n  Execution bca2c894747643adaa8dde28298137f1\n    Scheduled: 18:20:55.084782\n    Execution: 18:26:48.111309–18:26:54.083410\n    Cycle:     18:26:48.211302–18:26:54.054987\n    Start lateness: 353.026527 seconds\n\n  Execution f541196d23ff4dbeb831bc382a36f69d\n    Scheduled: 18:27:54.081170\n    Execution: 18:28:48.112355–18:28:53.975800\n    Cycle:     18:28:48.212316–18:28:53.945976\n    Start lateness: 54.031185 seconds\n\nSources: aggregate scheduler_executions, cycles and scheduler_outputs. The corresponding saved output paths are:\n\n  /var/home/mythos/.hermes/cron/output/fc6b7719f468/2026-10-11_11-19-55.md\n  /var/home/mythos/.hermes/cron/output/fc6b7719f468/2026-10-11_11-26-54.md\n  /var/home/mythos/.hermes/cron/output/fc6b7719f468/2026-10-11_11-28-53.md\n\nAll three executions have source=builtin, status=completed, error=null. Thus the nested collector’s “manually invoked” wording does not negate scheduler dispatch of its wrapper.\n\nHowever, schedule.minutes=1 produced observed cycle-start gaps of 420.003031 and 120.001014 seconds—not a continuous one-minute series. The aggregate explicitly records pause/resume and that the first cycle predates the timeout repair. The total first-start-to-last-finish span is only 545.737705 seconds, with gaps, not a nine-minute continuous soak. The precise contribution of pause/resume versus dispatch timing is not resolved by these records.\n\nJob fc6b7719f468 ended at repeat.completed=3 of times=3, enabled=false, state=completed, next_run_at=null. Its last_dispatch.kind=\"on_time\" coexists with lateness_seconds=53.4; that label is not proof of a tight monitoring deadline. delivery_outcome=\"suppressed\" and deliver=\"local\" also mean operator alert delivery was not demonstrated.\n\nRouting and health claims must remain narrow\n\nThe installed wrapper, /var/home/mythos/.hermes/scripts/arda_inference_scheduled_acceptance.py:17–29, explicitly requests:\n\n  model = openai_sub/gpt-6-astra\n  allow_forced_provider_fallback = false\n  workload_role = background\n  max_tokens = 1000\n  stream = false\n\nAll three retained responses identify openai_sub/gpt-6-astra in the background lane, with remote_elapsed_s approximately 5.572, 4.868 and 4.863. This establishes successful explicitly selected subscription execution and attribution. It does not demonstrate automatic local-to-remote escalation. Generic route-history fields such as origin_preference=\"auto\" and scoring summaries do not override the forced request; rejection records also contain model_role:forced_model_mismatch.\n\nAll fifteen endpoint observations were HTTP 200. They cover Prometheus /-/ready, Grafana /api/health, MinIO /minio/health/ready, and the two local model /health endpoints. A/bounded-server-check.py:24–31 reads at most 4096 bytes and records status/byte count; it does not validate application transactions or model output.\n\nAccordingly, all three interpretations correctly retained:\n\n  application_workload_health = null\n  backup_recoverability = null\n\nThey establish endpoint responses at the recorded instants—not scrape freshness, working Grafana queries, successful object storage operations, backup restoration, or inference quality. The Hermes assessment itself read an even older packet, dated approximately 09:34:55 UTC, rather than the overlapping 18:26 cycle.\n\nMissing failure-path evidence\n\n1. Endpoint failure through to an actionable retained outcome.\n   The collector catches endpoint exceptions as status=\"unavailable\", but its overall pass requires every endpoint to return 200 (A/bounded-server-check.py:27–29, 67–70). The wrapper asserts collector success before creating its interpretation packet (installed wrapper:14–15). An unhealthy endpoint therefore becomes an acceptance failure and skips interpretation, rather than demonstrating an ordinary monitoring incident path. All retained cycles are healthy-path examples.\n\n   Needed evidence: refusal/timeouts, HTTP 503/redirects, partial endpoint failure and recovery, with durable raw observations and a clear distinction between “service unhealthy” and “monitor failed.”\n\n2. Collector/transport failure and reliable cleanup.\n   Current code has SSH connection/subprocess timeouts and systemd limits, but these runs do not exercise a hung endpoint, resource-limit termination, SSH loss, cancellation or interrupted cleanup. Successful-run stop_exit=5 means “Unit … not loaded,” followed by inactive/MainPID=0—not proof of cancellation handling.\n\n   A/test_bounded_server_check.py contains mocked missing/503/duplicate/cleanup-read cases. Those are useful verdict tests, not end-to-end operational failure evidence.\n\n3. Interpreter unavailability or invalid output.\n   The wrapper has a 90-second URL timeout and checks facts, null unknowns, terminal completion and provider identity. Missing are installed-path demonstrations of subscription expiry, quota/cooldown, upstream stalls, malformed/truncated JSON, wrong facts, and recovery without unauthorized fallback.\n\n   Its limitation check accepts any nonempty string. Mechanical success cannot ensure that future limitation text is truthful or action-free. A/validate-installed-acceptance.py’s five corrupted controls test the file-task event validator, not these monitoring failure paths.\n\n4. Monitoring the monitor and notifying the operator.\n   No retained run demonstrates missed-fire detection, stale-evidence alarms, restart/reboot recovery, duplicate prevention, overlapping-cycle control, output-write failure, alert delivery, acknowledgement or recovery notification. Suppressed local delivery is insufficient for unattended awareness.\n\nDuration and provenance gaps\n\nA representative contiguous post-repair run is still needed, spanning normal operator use and background load, with explicit freshness/dispatch deadlines, latency distributions, resource growth, quota/cost accounting and recovery criteria. Three short successful interpretations cannot establish tail latency or continued availability.\n\nThere is also script-version uncertainty: today’s scheduled wrapper includes semantic_review_required, exact_fields and background_lane checks absent from the historical cycle records. The aggregate collector subsequently asserts exact answer fields and background headers (A/collect-installed-acceptance.py:26–35), but that does not prove those checks existed in the executed wrapper version. No executed-script hash is supplied in these records. The independent review explicitly covered the first two cycles; the final third cycle and scheduler correlation were parent-verified.\n\nRecommendation\n\nRetain the existing disposition in P:94–103: desktop inference off, capped deterministic endpoint collection, and explicitly selected subscription interpretation under review. Do not label this unattended automatic monitoring or automatic role selection.\n\nThe next qualification should target failure reporting, stale/missed-run detection, delivered alerts and a contiguous post-repair duration test—not repeat the resolved parser repair. Application-health and backup-recovery qualification can remain separate, provided endpoint monitoring is honestly labelled.\n\nUnresolved uncertainties are dispatch behavior across pause/resume, actual simultaneous upstream compute, behavior under endpoint/provider failure, reliable notification and recovery, executed-script provenance, and sustained resource/latency/quota behavior. None of the retained observations establishes current availability.", "timestamp": 1791745886691}
